This is the second post in my series on extending Lua with Zig.
In [the previous post]($link.page('blog/extend-lua-with-zig-1')),
we talked about Hello World in Zig and wrote our first `build.zig` script.
In this post our goal is to run Hello World again, but via Lua hosted by Zig.

# Adding Ziglua

We'll work with [Ziglua](https://github.com/natecraddock/ziglua),
a fantastic project that Nate Craddock has maintained since 2022.
Ziglua provides Zig bindings for the Lua C API.
Zig has excellent native ability to work with C libraries (like Lua),
so in a very strict sense, Ziglua is not necessary for what we'll do.
The work that Ziglua does is translating Lua's C conventions for naming, errors and so forth
into a more "Ziggy" style and making use of the language's affordances.

In cases where I depend on a C library in a Zig project,
I've often used Ziglua as an example
for the kind of work I can do to make my life using the library better.
If you get the itch, I highly recommend cracking open Ziglua's source.

## Fetch

To add Ziglua to our project, we'll run
```bash
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/natecraddock/ziglua
```

This does a couple things:
+ We resolve the URL to a commit and to a tarball to download.
+ The tarball is downloaded to a directory called `zig-pkg` (and stripped, more later)
+ Information about the Zig package is written to a file called `build.zig.zon`

If you didn't have a `build.zig.zon` file, well now you do.
Running the above in a directory without a `build.zig.zon` results in approximately the following file

```zon
// build.zig.zon
// NB: the fingerprint field depends on the name field,
// the name field will be derived from the name of the directory it lives in.
.{
    .name = .hello_lua,
    .version = "0.0.1",
    .dependencies = .{
        .zlua = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/natecraddock/ziglua#8b2c14e0f9f89a9407dd7dd91752c7d8fd03cb62",
            .hash = "zlua-0.1.0-hGRpC8yYBQAtI5hy60lb1vK9Tc7s-Ouu0aMFe2Kdc3_s",
        },
    },
    .minimum_zig_version = "0.17.0",
    .paths = .{""},
    .fingerprint = 0x3c2bc4659f2dcf49, // changing this has trust and security implications
}
```

## `build.zig.zon` also ZON

Think of `build.zig.zon` as a kind of package manifest.
You can see it lists some basic info about our project,
like the name (which is, potentially confusingly, an "enum literal" rather than a string)
